as a function of time.

1377
00:50:51,040 --> 00:50:55,040
That gives you a complete waveform, not

1378
00:50:52,880 --> 00:50:57,040
just a single data point. Then you send

1379
00:50:55,040 --> 00:50:59,200
a second pulse and again you record the

1380
00:50:57,040 --> 00:51:01,359
returned signal. By the time the second

1381
00:50:59,200 --> 00:51:03,680
pulse arrives, the scatterers such as

1382
00:51:01,359 --> 00:51:06,720
red blood cells will have moved slightly

1383
00:51:03,680 --> 00:51:08,559
compared to their original positions.

1384
00:51:06,720 --> 00:51:09,920
To find out how much motion occurred, we

1385
00:51:08,559 --> 00:51:12,000
compare the two signals using

1386
00:51:09,920 --> 00:51:13,839
correlation. You look for the time delay

1387
00:51:12,000 --> 00:51:15,839
denoted as toao where the two signals

1388
00:51:13,839 --> 00:51:17,680
are most similar.

1389
00:51:15,839 --> 00:51:19,280
This time delay tells you how far the

1390
00:51:17,680 --> 00:51:21,119
scatterers have moved between the two

1391
00:51:19,280 --> 00:51:23,200
measurements.

1392
00:51:21,119 --> 00:51:25,280
When you multiply that toao by the sound

1393
00:51:23,200 --> 00:51:27,920
speed along the beam direction, you get

1394
00:51:25,280 --> 00:51:29,680
the relative displacement. From that you

1395
00:51:27,920 --> 00:51:31,359
can calculate the velocity or even

1396
00:51:29,680 --> 00:51:32,880
derive the Doppler frequency if you

1397
00:51:31,359 --> 00:51:34,880
prefer.

1398
00:51:32,880 --> 00:51:37,040
So this is the basic idea behind the

1399
00:51:34,880 --> 00:51:39,280
correlation method. It's simple but very

1400
00:51:37,040 --> 00:51:41,359
effective. By comparing the returning

1401
00:51:39,280 --> 00:51:43,200
waveforms over time, we can accurately

1402
00:51:41,359 --> 00:51:45,520
measure how fast and in what direction

1403
00:51:43,200 --> 00:51:47,839
the blood or tissue is moving. This is

1404
00:51:45,520 --> 00:51:52,200
often used alongside Doppler analysis to

1405
00:51:47,839 --> 00:51:52,200
enhance motion estimation accuracy.

1406
00:51:52,720 --> 00:51:56,319
Now, this slide shows how the Doppler

1407
00:51:54,720 --> 00:51:58,400
effect can be used to measure and

1408
00:51:56,319 --> 00:51:59,920
visualize velocity.

1409
00:51:58,400 --> 00:52:01,760
The Doppler information can be

1410
00:51:59,920 --> 00:52:03,599
superimposed directly on the B mode

1411
00:52:01,760 --> 00:52:05,760
image. The B mode gives you the

1412
00:52:03,599 --> 00:52:07,440
anatomical structure. For example, you

1413
00:52:05,760 --> 00:52:10,480
can clearly see a large blood vessel

1414
00:52:07,440 --> 00:52:12,079
here. Once you have that anatomical

1415
00:52:10,480 --> 00:52:15,520
background, you can add the velocity

1416
00:52:12,079 --> 00:52:17,599
field on top of it using color coding.

1417
00:52:15,520 --> 00:52:20,240
In color Doppler imaging, the motion is

1418
00:52:17,599 --> 00:52:22,559
encoded using colors, typically the RGB

1419
00:52:20,240 --> 00:52:24,559
scheme. Motion toward the transducer is

1420
00:52:22,559 --> 00:52:27,520
displayed in red, while motion away from

1421
00:52:24,559 --> 00:52:29,680
the transducer is shown in blue.

1422
00:52:27,520 --> 00:52:31,599
Sometimes a green component is used to

1423
00:52:29,680 --> 00:52:33,280
indicate the variance of the signal.

1424
00:52:31,599 --> 00:52:36,400
That is how much fluctuation or

1425
00:52:33,280 --> 00:52:38,480
turbulence there is in the flow. If the

1426
00:52:36,400 --> 00:52:40,559
blood flow is smooth and uniform, you

1427
00:52:38,480 --> 00:52:42,640
won't see much green at all. But if the

1428
00:52:40,559 --> 00:52:44,400
flow becomes turbulent or irregular,

1429
00:52:42,640 --> 00:52:45,920
you'll see more green appearing in the

1430
00:52:44,400 --> 00:52:47,920
image.

1431
00:52:45,920 --> 00:52:50,079
This color map gives a one-dimensional

1432
00:52:47,920 --> 00:52:52,400
view of motion only along the direction

1433
00:52:50,079 --> 00:52:54,480
of the ultrasound beam. It doesn't tell

1434
00:52:52,400 --> 00:52:56,480
you about lateral movement, but it's

1435
00:52:54,480 --> 00:52:58,000
very effective for examining flow in

1436
00:52:56,480 --> 00:53:00,079
vessels.

1437
00:52:58,000 --> 00:53:02,400
In more advanced cases, you can apply a

1438
00:53:00,079 --> 00:53:04,480
FIA transform to the Doppler signal to

1439
00:53:02,400 --> 00:53:06,400
obtain the velocity distribution because

1440
00:53:04,480 --> 00:53:08,079
the motion is not always at a single

1441
00:53:06,400 --> 00:53:10,160
frequency.

1442
00:53:08,079 --> 00:53:12,480
Blood flow, for instance, has multiple

1443
00:53:10,160 --> 00:53:14,960
velocity components due to pulsation and

1444
00:53:12,480 --> 00:53:16,960
vessel geometry. So, color Doppler

1445
00:53:14,960 --> 00:53:18,800
provides a dynamic intuitive way to

1446
00:53:16,960 --> 00:53:23,480
visualize those variations on top of

1447
00:53:18,800 --> 00:53:23,480
anatomical images. Here

1448
00:53:24,000 --> 00:53:28,640
we move to spectral Doppler imaging as

1449
00:53:25,920 --> 00:53:30,400
shown in figure 323 from your textbook.

1450
00:53:28,640 --> 00:53:31,839
This method provides more detailed

1451
00:53:30,400 --> 00:53:34,559
information about the range of

1452
00:53:31,839 --> 00:53:36,240
velocities within a vessel.

1453
00:53:34,559 --> 00:53:38,559
You can see how the signal amplitude

1454
00:53:36,240 --> 00:53:40,640
varies over the cardiac cycle during

1455
00:53:38,559 --> 00:53:43,920
systol when the heart contracts and

1456
00:53:40,640 --> 00:53:46,000
diastol when it relaxes. During systol

1457
00:53:43,920 --> 00:53:48,240
the flow velocity is higher which shifts

1458
00:53:46,000 --> 00:53:50,559
the doppler frequency upward. During

1459
00:53:48,240 --> 00:53:53,200
diastol the flow slows down. So the

1460
00:53:50,559 --> 00:53:55,200
Doppler shift becomes smaller.

1461
00:53:53,200 --> 00:53:57,200
The result is a spectrum showing how

1462
00:53:55,200 --> 00:53:59,760
signal amplitude changes with Doppler

1463
00:53:57,200 --> 00:54:01,280
frequency over time. Each vertical line

1464
00:53:59,760 --> 00:54:03,040
in the display represents the

1465
00:54:01,280 --> 00:54:05,920
distribution of frequencies or

1466
00:54:03,040 --> 00:54:07,920
velocities at a given instant.

1467
00:54:05,920 --> 00:54:10,000
When plotted together, this forms the

1468
00:54:07,920 --> 00:54:12,480
spectral waveform which you often see in

1469
00:54:10,000 --> 00:54:14,480
clinical Doppler displays. By analyzing

1470
00:54:12,480 --> 00:54:16,400
these patterns, clinicians can assess

1471
00:54:14,480 --> 00:54:19,680
blood flow speed, direction, and

1472
00:54:16,400 --> 00:54:21,920
uniformity through the cardiac cycle.

1473
00:54:19,680 --> 00:54:24,079
So to summarize, Doppler imaging

1474
00:54:21,920 --> 00:54:25,839
includes several complimentary modes.

1475
00:54:24,079 --> 00:54:28,079
Continuous wave, pulseed wave and

1476
00:54:25,839 --> 00:54:30,319
correlation methods along with color and

1477
00:54:28,079 --> 00:54:32,000
spectral Doppler.

1478
00:54:30,319 --> 00:54:34,400
Continuous and pulse modes measure

1479
00:54:32,000 --> 00:54:36,240
velocity. Color Doppler visualizes it

1480
00:54:34,400 --> 00:54:38,559
and spectral Doppler reveals its

1481
00:54:36,240 --> 00:54:40,319
frequency content. Together they provide

1482
00:54:38,559 --> 00:54:42,720
a comprehensive view of motion and flow

1483
00:54:40,319 --> 00:54:45,720
in ultrasound imaging anatomically and

1484
00:54:42,720 --> 00:54:45,720
dynamically.

1485
00:54:47,359 --> 00:54:51,359
Finally, let's talk briefly about

1486
00:54:49,119 --> 00:54:53,520
ultrasound image artifacts. Like any

1487
00:54:51,359 --> 00:54:55,760
imaging modality, ultrasound is not

1488
00:54:53,520 --> 00:54:58,079
perfect and various artifacts can appear

1489
00:54:55,760 --> 00:55:00,880
due to limitations in the system or the

1490
00:54:58,079 --> 00:55:02,240
physics of sound propagation.

1491
00:55:00,880 --> 00:55:04,720
These artifacts may come from

1492
00:55:02,240 --> 00:55:07,040
reflection, refraction, attenuation or

1493
00:55:04,720 --> 00:55:09,280
motion. Some are simply distortions

1494
00:55:07,040 --> 00:55:11,119
while others can mimic real anatomical

1495
00:55:09,280 --> 00:55:12,880
structures.

1496
00:55:11,119 --> 00:55:15,040
Understanding and recognizing these

1497
00:55:12,880 --> 00:55:16,960
artifacts is extremely important because

1498
00:55:15,040 --> 00:55:18,480
they can affect how you interpret the

1499
00:55:16,960 --> 00:55:20,000
image.

1500
00:55:18,480 --> 00:55:22,079
We'll look at examples of these

1501
00:55:20,000 --> 00:55:24,160
artifacts and discuss what causes them

1502
00:55:22,079 --> 00:55:28,760
and how they can sometimes even be used

1503
00:55:24,160 --> 00:55:28,760
advantageously in image interpretation.

1504
00:55:30,640 --> 00:55:34,400
Now let's talk about time gain

1505
00:55:32,319 --> 00:55:36,319
compensation. When we deal with very

1506
00:55:34,400 --> 00:55:38,079
high frequency ultrasound ultrasound

1507
00:55:36,319 --> 00:55:41,760
signals in the megahertz range, the

1508
00:55:38,079 --> 00:55:43,680
signal amplitude can be extremely small.

1509
00:55:41,760 --> 00:55:46,319
If we simply record the raw signal

1510
00:55:43,680 --> 00:55:48,079
amplitude or intensity as it is without

1511
00:55:46,319 --> 00:55:50,319
doing any correction, you'll find that

1512
00:55:48,079 --> 00:55:52,240
the deeper features inside the tissue

1513
00:55:50,319 --> 00:55:54,079
appear much weaker than the ones near

1514
00:55:52,240 --> 00:55:56,160
the surface.

1515
00:55:54,079 --> 00:55:58,400
This happens because of attenuation and

1516
00:55:56,160 --> 00:56:00,240
wave scattering. As the ultrasound wave

1517
00:55:58,400 --> 00:56:02,480
travels deeper, part of the energy is

1518
00:56:00,240 --> 00:56:04,640
absorbed and part of it is scattered in

1519
00:56:02,480 --> 00:56:06,720
different directions.

1520
00:56:04,640 --> 00:56:09,119
Both effects cause the returning signal,

1521
00:56:06,720 --> 00:56:11,680
the echo, to decay exponentially with

1522
00:56:09,119 --> 00:56:13,839
depth. Near the surface, the reflected

1523
00:56:11,680 --> 00:56:15,680
wave is strong, but as you move deeper,

1524
00:56:13,839 --> 00:56:18,160
the signal strength drops off very

1525
00:56:15,680 --> 00:56:20,240
quickly. The decay roughly follows an

1526
00:56:18,160 --> 00:56:22,079
exponential curve.

1527
00:56:20,240 --> 00:56:24,240
To correct for this loss, we apply

1528
00:56:22,079 --> 00:56:27,040
what's called time gain compensation or

1529
00:56:24,240 --> 00:56:29,280
TGC. The idea is simple. As the echo

1530
00:56:27,040 --> 00:56:31,280
arrives later in time, which corresponds

1531
00:56:29,280 --> 00:56:33,920
to greater depth, you gradually increase

1532
00:56:31,280 --> 00:56:36,400
the amplification factor.

1533
00:56:33,920 --> 00:56:38,079
In other words, you boost the gain as a

1534
00:56:36,400 --> 00:56:40,400
function of depth to counteract the

1535
00:56:38,079 --> 00:56:42,319
exponential decay. The amplification

1536
00:56:40,400 --> 00:56:44,480
curve roughly follows the opposite shape

1537
00:56:42,319 --> 00:56:46,240
of the attenuation curve. When you

1538
00:56:44,480 --> 00:56:47,920
multiply the received signal by this

1539
00:56:46,240 --> 00:56:49,520
depth dependent gain, the resulting

1540
00:56:47,920 --> 00:56:51,200
image becomes more uniform in

1541
00:56:49,520 --> 00:56:53,200
brightness.

1542
00:56:51,200 --> 00:56:54,799
This compensation makes the ultrasound

1543
00:56:53,200 --> 00:56:57,839
image look much better and more

1544
00:56:54,799 --> 00:56:59,520
interpretable. Without it, the deeper

1545
00:56:57,839 --> 00:57:01,200
regions would appear dark and faint,

1546
00:56:59,520 --> 00:57:03,760
while the shallow regions would look

1547
00:57:01,200 --> 00:57:05,920
overly bright. With TGC, you recover a

1548
00:57:03,760 --> 00:57:08,079
more balanced image.

1549
00:57:05,920 --> 00:57:10,000
However, remember that this correction

1550
00:57:08,079 --> 00:57:12,240
is based on assumptions about tissue

1551
00:57:10,000 --> 00:57:14,480
homogeneity. It works well when the

1552
00:57:12,240 --> 00:57:16,720
tissue properties are roughly uniform.

1553
00:57:14,480 --> 00:57:18,960
In real biological tissue, conditions

1554
00:57:16,720 --> 00:57:22,319
are not perfectly homogeneous. So, the

1555
00:57:18,960 --> 00:57:24,240
quantitative accuracy is not perfect.

1556
00:57:22,319 --> 00:57:26,240
It's similar to what happens in CT

1557
00:57:24,240 --> 00:57:28,400
imaging when we apply beam hardening

1558
00:57:26,240 --> 00:57:31,200
correction. We can reduce the artifacts,

1559
00:57:28,400 --> 00:57:33,280
but it's never a perfect fix.

1560
00:57:31,200 --> 00:57:35,119
So keep in mind that in ultrasound,

1561
00:57:33,280 --> 00:57:36,559
different echo times correspond to

1562
00:57:35,119 --> 00:57:38,640
different depths, and the deeper the

1563
00:57:36,559 --> 00:57:40,799
echo, the stronger the amplification you

1564
00:57:38,640 --> 00:57:44,359
need to apply. That's the principle of

1565
00:57:40,799 --> 00:57:44,359
time gain compensation.

1566
00:57:46,160 --> 00:57:49,520
Now let's talk about the next type of

1567
00:57:47,760 --> 00:57:51,680
artifact, which is caused by what we

1568
00:57:49,520 --> 00:57:53,839
call side lobes.

1569
00:57:51,680 --> 00:57:55,680
In ultrasound imaging, no matter how you

1570
00:57:53,839 --> 00:57:57,520
modulate the transducer elements,

1571
00:57:55,680 --> 00:57:59,440
whether you use a one-dimensional or a

1572
00:57:57,520 --> 00:58:01,440
two-dimensional array or even a single

1573
00:57:59,440 --> 00:58:04,240
transducer, you can never create a

1574
00:58:01,440 --> 00:58:06,240
perfectly straight narrow beam.

1575
00:58:04,240 --> 00:58:08,240
Each transducer element generates a

1576
00:58:06,240 --> 00:58:10,160
spherical wave. And when these waves

1577
00:58:08,240 --> 00:58:12,319
combine through interference, they form

1578
00:58:10,160 --> 00:58:14,319
one strong main beam along the principal

1579
00:58:12,319 --> 00:58:17,359
direction plus several weaker beams on

1580
00:58:14,319 --> 00:58:19,119
the sides. These are the side lobes.

1581
00:58:17,359 --> 00:58:21,200
Physically, this happens because we're

1582
00:58:19,119 --> 00:58:23,599
dealing with waves and waves naturally

1583
00:58:21,200 --> 00:58:25,920
spread out. Even if we adjust the phase

1584
00:58:23,599 --> 00:58:28,400
and amplitude across the array, we can't

1585
00:58:25,920 --> 00:58:31,680
perfectly confine all the energy into a

1586
00:58:28,400 --> 00:58:33,280
single pencil thin line. The best we can

1587
00:58:31,680 --> 00:58:35,280
achieve is a main lobe with high

1588
00:58:33,280 --> 00:58:38,000
intensity surrounded by smaller side

1589
00:58:35,280 --> 00:58:40,160
loes with lower intensity.

1590
00:58:38,000 --> 00:58:41,760
If you've studied forier analysis, this

1591
00:58:40,160 --> 00:58:43,920
is similar to what happens when you

1592
00:58:41,760 --> 00:58:46,480
approximate a sharp function. You always

1593
00:58:43,920 --> 00:58:48,079
get some rippling known as ringing. Here

1594
00:58:46,480 --> 00:58:51,200
the same principle applies to the

1595
00:58:48,079 --> 00:58:52,960
ultrasound beam. Now these side lobes

1596
00:58:51,200 --> 00:58:55,119
can create misleading signals in the

1597
00:58:52,960 --> 00:58:57,200
image. For example, if you're scanning a

1598
00:58:55,119 --> 00:58:59,280
region with a single bright reflector,

1599
00:58:57,200 --> 00:59:01,359
the main lobe gives a strong echo at the

1600
00:58:59,280 --> 00:59:03,119
correct location.

1601
00:59:01,359 --> 00:59:04,960
But the side lobes can also pick up

1602
00:59:03,119 --> 00:59:07,119
reflections from off-axis points which

1603
00:59:04,960 --> 00:59:09,119
then appear as false echoes in the image

1604
00:59:07,119 --> 00:59:10,960
often as smaller or dimmer copies of the

1605
00:59:09,119 --> 00:59:12,799
real structure.

1606
00:59:10,960 --> 00:59:14,720
So you might see multiple bright spots

1607
00:59:12,799 --> 00:59:16,799
even though only one real reflector

1608
00:59:14,720 --> 00:59:19,040
exists. In clinical practice,

1609
00:59:16,799 --> 00:59:21,119
recognizing this artifact is essential.

1610
00:59:19,040 --> 00:59:23,280
You may move the transducer slightly to

1611
00:59:21,119 --> 00:59:25,520
confirm which echo is genuine and which

1612
00:59:23,280 --> 00:59:27,760
is an artifact.

1613
00:59:25,520 --> 00:59:29,520
Another related effect is reverberation

1614
00:59:27,760 --> 00:59:31,200
which happens when echoes bounce back

1615
00:59:29,520 --> 00:59:33,680
and forth between two reflective

1616
00:59:31,200 --> 00:59:35,839
surfaces such as tissue layers or an air

1617
00:59:33,680 --> 00:59:37,920
tissue interface.

1618
00:59:35,839 --> 00:59:39,839
These multiple reflections cause several

1619
00:59:37,920 --> 00:59:41,920
delayed echoes to appear on the image

1620
00:59:39,839 --> 00:59:43,680
even though only one actual surface

1621
00:59:41,920 --> 00:59:46,079
exists.

1622
00:59:43,680 --> 00:59:48,079
They appear as repeated evenly spaced

1623
00:59:46,079 --> 00:59:50,000
lines decreasing in intensity with

1624
00:59:48,079 --> 00:59:52,000
depth. You can see examples of these

1625
00:59:50,000 --> 00:59:54,400
side lobe patterns and reverberation

1626
00:59:52,000 --> 00:59:56,160
artifacts in the slide images here.

1627
00:59:54,400 --> 00:59:57,920
Understanding them helps you identify

1628
00:59:56,160 --> 00:59:59,760
what's real and what's simply an effect

1629
00:59:57,920 --> 01:00:02,760
of wave interference and multiple

1630
00:59:59,760 --> 01:00:02,760
reflection.

1631
01:00:04,240 --> 01:00:08,400
Let's continue with more examples of

1632
01:00:06,000 --> 01:00:10,480
ultrasound artifacts. One common type is

1633
01:00:08,400 --> 01:00:13,040
caused by multiple reflections between

1634
01:00:10,480 --> 01:00:16,079
layers. Imagine sending one ultrasound

1635
01:00:13,040 --> 01:00:17,839
pulse into a structure like the lung.

1636
01:00:16,079 --> 01:00:20,319
The wave first reflects from the top

1637
01:00:17,839 --> 01:00:22,160
surface, giving you the first echo. Some

1638
01:00:20,319 --> 01:00:24,960
energy penetrates deeper, hits the

1639
01:00:22,160 --> 01:00:26,880
bottom surface, and reflects.

1640
01:00:24,960 --> 01:00:28,720
But on its way, that echo may bounce

1641
01:00:26,880 --> 01:00:31,440
again from the top surface and return

1642
01:00:28,720 --> 01:00:33,359
once more to the transducer.

1643
01:00:31,440 --> 01:00:35,520
As a result, even though you sent only

1644
01:00:33,359 --> 01:00:37,599
one pulse, you detect several echoes

1645
01:00:35,520 --> 01:00:39,839
arriving at different times. This

1646
01:00:37,599 --> 01:00:41,680
produces a sequence of repeated bands on

1647
01:00:39,839 --> 01:00:44,160
the image, very similar to hearing

1648
01:00:41,680 --> 01:00:46,799
multiple hellos echoing in a mountain

